        <div id="built-in-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Honest Math on Built-In Solar Panels
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical solar power bank has a panel roughly the size of a phone screen on its face. Sunlight at midday delivers around 1,000 watts per square meter at best, and good phone-sized panels convert about 20 percent of that. Do the arithmetic and a panel that size can produce a watt or two at the absolute peak, with the bank lying flat in full summer sun and pointed straight at the sky.
          </p>

          <p>
            Real conditions are worse. The sun moves, so a bank lying on a table is only aimed well for part of the day. Panels lose output as they heat up, and a black power bank on a hot surface gets very hot. Clouds, haze, shade from trees and low winter sun each cut output further. Then the battery's charging circuit loses some of what the panel does make. The result is that in typical use, a built-in panel adds only a few percent of a 10,000mAh battery per day.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Built-In vs Folding Panel</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-2W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical built-in panel at peak</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Few %</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Charge added per day, typical use</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-15W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Real output, 28-30W folding panel</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Phone charge from folding panel, strong sun</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Put another way, filling a 10,000mAh solar bank from empty using only its own panel can take many days of sun, and a 20,000mAh or 25,000mAh solar bank with the same small panel takes proportionally longer. That is why solar charging on these banks is best thought of as an emergency backup. The marketing images of a bank charging on a sunny rock suggest something much more capable than the physics allows.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes built-in solar banks useless. They are fine power banks that happen to have a slow trickle charger attached, and that trickle can matter in an emergency when every percent counts. Some models add useful extras like a flashlight, a wireless pad and a rugged shell. The mistake is buying one expecting solar to replace the wall charger. Charge it from the wall, and treat the panel as a bonus.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be especially skeptical of large capacity claims on built-in solar banks. Listings advertising 30,000 to 50,000mAh from unfamiliar brands often overstate capacity, and a big battery with a tiny panel only makes the solar part less meaningful. If a product's main selling point is the panel, look at the folding options below instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-wins" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why a Folding Panel Plus a Bank Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            A folding solar charger spreads three to six panels across a fabric or plastic frame, giving it ten to twenty times the surface area of a built-in panel. A 28 to 30W folding panel like the Anker SOLIX PS30 or BigBlue 28W can deliver 10 to 15W to a phone in strong sun, which is enough to charge it directly in a couple of hours, the same order of speed as a basic wall charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing that panel with a separate power bank solves the obvious problem: you need power at night, and the sun is out during the day. The panel charges the bank while you hike, fish or sit at camp, and the bank charges your phone in the tent. It also protects your phone. Phones charging directly from a panel can stop and start every time a cloud passes, and many will not resume charging on their own, while a power bank buffers that.
          </p>

