        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Built-in solar power banks are ordinary batteries with a slow trickle charger attached. Buy one like the BLAVOR if you want a self-contained glovebox or go-bag item, charge it from the wall, and do not count on the panel for more than a few percent a day. That is the honest version of a product that is usually marketed as free, endless power.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you want solar that keeps phones charged, buy a folding panel and a separate bank: the Anker SOLIX PS30 with the Anker 737 for most people, or the BigBlue 28W with the Anker Nano for hikers. And when your list grows to a CPAP, a router or anything with a wall plug, step up to a 100W panel and a small power station. Size the panel and battery to each other, and the sun will do the rest.
